 "sourceStatement": "Mencius 孟子 (Mengzi, c. 372–289 BC) and his disciples, 孟子 (the Mencius), {CITE}, from 〈{PIAN}〉, in the received text as printed in Zhu Xi 朱熹, 孟子集注 (part of his 四書章句集注, completed c. 1190), from the Wenyuange 文淵閣 copy of the Siku Quanshu 四庫全書 (1782), as transcribed by the Kanseki Repository (Kanripo, KR1h0015). Collated character by character against Zhao Qi's 趙岐 孟子章句 (2nd century) in the Sibu Congkan 四部叢刊 photographic reprint of a Song edition (Kanripo KR1h0002). Readings that change the sense are noted in the notes; graphic variants are not. Chapter divisions and their numbering are Zhu Xi's, the standard ones; Zhu Xi's text is substantially the one James Legge printed in The Chinese Classics, vol. 2 (Hong Kong, 1861). All punctuation is editorial — the original has none. Characters are traditional, as the edition prints them. Translations are made fresh from the Chinese. Pronunciation convention: modern Mandarin, given in Hanyu Pinyin — an admitted anachronism, and the living convention for reading these texts. Any prepared audio is synthesized speech in that same modern Mandarin convention; the exact voice used is recorded in audio/<id>/voice.txt and is fixed for every Chinese text.",
 "cutRule": "the fourteen 篇 (Books 1A–7B) cut into parts of whole chapters, ~1,000 characters; within a part, one unit per stretch between Zhu Xi's commentary interruptions; each chapter starts a card (ln = chapter number within the 篇, p: true)",
